  "question": "Switch to database concurrency: your overlap constructions preserved all counts; now test whether transactions preserve an invariant. PostgreSQL has two doctor rows, Alice and Bob, both marked on call. At repeatable-read isolation, each transaction counts on-call doctors and, if the count exceeds one, marks its own doctor off call. Alice and Bob run concurrently. Show an exact interleaving where both commit and nobody remains on call. Why does updating different rows matter? [Interviewer pause: one point five seconds.] Provide executable SQL using serializable isolation that preserves at least one on-call doctor, specify exactly what must be retried after a serialization failure, and justify correctness.",
  "recognizedQuestion": "switch to database concurrency your overlap constructions preserved all counts Now test whether transactions preserve an invariant PostgreSQL has two doctor rows, Alice and Bob both marked on call at repeatable read isolation each transaction counts on call doctors and if the count exceeds one marks its own doctor off call Alice and Bob run concurrently show an exact interleaving where both commit and nobody remains on call Why does updating different rows matter Interviewer pause one point five seconds provide executable SQL using serializable isolation that preserves at least one uncall doctor Specify exactly what must be retried after a serialization failure and justify correctness",
